from .common import pick, truth, written

NOTES = {
    1: ['The whole deck answers one question: when many stations share one link, who gets to send, and when? There are three families of answer. **Random access** (the CSMA family): nobody is in charge and stations compete, so collisions can happen. **Controlled access** (reservation, polling, token passing): stations take turns, so there are no collisions. **Channelization** (FDMA, TDMA, CDMA): the channel is cut into pieces by frequency, time or code. Two letters come up throughout: **R** is the channel’s full rate in bps and **M** is the number of stations, so **R/M** is one station’s fair, equal share.'],
    2: ['The three families of multiple-access protocols. Keep their spirit in mind: **random access** = free for all (anyone may try, collisions are possible); **controlled access** = take turns (one station at a time, decided in an orderly way); **channelization** = split the channel (each station gets its own slice).'],
    3: ['A **broadcast (shared) link** has many sending and receiving nodes using a **single shared medium**, so every node hears every transmission. Examples: the shared cable of classic Ethernet, the radio channel of a Wi-Fi network, a satellite channel, and the radio of a 4G/5G cell. Compare with a point-to-point link, where only two devices use the line and there is nothing to coordinate.'],
    4: ['The problem with sharing: if two or more nodes transmit at the same time, their signals add together on the medium and both frames are destroyed. That is a **collision**, and the bandwidth used during it is **wasted**. So the nodes need some way to coordinate who sends when.',
        'The solution is a protocol that coordinates the active nodes. These are called **Medium (or Multiple) Access Control protocols**, MAC protocols, and they belong to the **MAC sublayer**, the lower part of the data link layer. (MAC here is the same “MAC” as in MAC address: both belong to this sublayer.)'],
    5: ['In these slides the data link layer is split into two sublayers. The upper one, **data link control** (called **LLC**, Logical Link Control), handles **error control and flow control**. The lower one, **multiple-access control (MAC)**, handles **framing**, **MAC addressing** and **deciding who may use the shared medium**. Different LANs (Ethernet, Wi-Fi) have different MAC sublayers but can share the same LLC.'],
    6: ['The family tree. **Random access**: **CSMA/CD** (collision detection, used on **wired** LANs such as classic Ethernet) and **CSMA/CA** (collision avoidance, used on **wireless** LANs, Wi-Fi, because a radio can’t listen while it transmits). **Controlled access**: **reservation**, **polling** and **token passing**. **Channelization**: **FDMA** (by frequency), **TDMA** (by time) and **CDMA** (by code). Exams often name one protocol and ask for its family.'],
    7: ['In **random access**, **no station is superior** to another and none controls another: there is no master and no schedule. A station with a frame to send **uses the link directly**, following the protocol’s own rules for deciding whether to send right now. Because each station decides on its own, two may decide to send at the same moment, so collisions are possible. The protocols differ in how they reduce that risk and what they do after a collision.'],
    8: ['**CSMA** (Carrier Sense Multiple Access) means **listen before you talk**: a station **senses the medium** first, and only sends if nobody else is transmitting. It relies on the fact that in a LAN the propagation time is very small, so when one station starts sending, the others hear it almost immediately and wait.',
        'Sensing **reduces** collisions but **cannot eliminate** them. The reason is propagation delay: when station B starts sending, its signal takes some time to reach station C. During that time C hears silence, thinks the medium is free, and starts too, and the two frames collide somewhere in the middle (the figure shows this).',
        'So a collision can only happen if two stations start **within one propagation time** of each other. If C starts after B’s signal has reached it, C will hear B and wait.'],
    9: ['The **vulnerable time** of CSMA, the window in which a collision can still happen, equals the **maximum propagation time** (t_prop): how long a bit takes to travel between the **two stations farthest apart** on the link. If station A starts at t = 0, the farthest station B only hears the first bit at t = t_prop; until then, B might start sending too.'],
    10: ['The **longer the propagation delay**, the **worse** CSMA performs, because the vulnerable window gets longer. During it, a distant node **thinks the channel is still idle** and may start sending. In the figure, B starts at t1; C and D sense the channel before B’s signal reaches them, find it idle, and may transmit. This is why CSMA networks limit cable length.'],
    11: ['All CSMA protocols sense before sending, but they differ in the two questions on the slide: what does a station do when the medium is **idle**, and what does it do when it is **busy**? The three answers are **non-persistent**, **1-persistent** and **p-persistent** CSMA. “Persistent” means how insistently the station keeps listening while the medium is busy.'],
    12: ['**Non-persistent** CSMA: sense the medium. If it is **idle**, transmit. If it is **busy**, **back off**: wait a **random** amount of time without listening, then sense again. Non-persistent stations are **deferential**: they politely walk away from a busy line instead of waiting at it.',
         'Performance: because waiting stations pick **different random delays**, they are unlikely to return at the same moment, so there are **fewer collisions**. The cost is **wasted bandwidth**: when the current transmission ends, everyone may still be in the middle of their random wait, so the medium sits **idle** even though stations have frames to send. The longer the backoff, the more time is wasted.'],
    13: ['**1-persistent** CSMA was designed to avoid that idle time. Sense the medium. If it is **idle**, transmit immediately. If it is **busy**, **keep listening continuously**, and the moment it becomes idle, transmit immediately, with **probability 1** (always).',
         '1-persistent stations are called **selfish**: they grab the channel as soon as they can.',
         'The weakness: if **two or more** stations were waiting for the same busy channel, they all see it go idle at the same instant and all send at once, so a **collision is guaranteed**. Under heavy load that happens all the time, which is why 1-persistent has the worst throughput on slide 16.'],
    14: ['**p-persistent** CSMA divides time into **slots**, each typically equal to the **maximum propagation delay**. Sense the medium. If it is **idle**, transmit with **probability p**, or with probability **1 - p** wait **one time slot** and sense again. For example, with p = 0.25, a station sends in a given slot with a 25% chance and waits with a 75% chance, like rolling dice each slot.',
         'If the medium is **busy**, keep **listening continuously** until it is idle, then apply step 1 (the probability rule).',
         'It combines the two others’ strengths: it **reduces collisions like non-persistent** (waiting stations don’t all jump in at once, since each only sends with probability p) and **reduces idle time like 1-persistent** (stations keep listening instead of walking away).'],
    15: ['The flow charts summarise the three methods. **1-persistent**: sense until idle, then send. **Non-persistent**: if busy, wait a random time and sense again; if idle, send. **p-persistent**: wait until idle, then draw a random number R between 0 and 1: if **R ≤ p**, send; otherwise wait one slot and repeat. In CSMA/CD, all three continue the same way after sending: watch for a collision and handle it.'],
    16: ['The graph plots **throughput** (the fraction of time the channel carries successful frames) against **load** (how much traffic stations try to send). At heavy load, **1-persistent** is the **lowest**, because waiting stations all collide; **0.5-persistent** is a bit better, then **0.1-persistent**, then **non-persistent**, and **0.01-persistent** is the highest. The smaller p, the more cautious the stations, and the fewer collisions under heavy load.'],
    17: ['Plain CSMA has an inefficiency: once a collision happens, the stations involved keep sending their **whole frames**, even though those frames are already ruined, so the channel stays unusable until both finish. **CSMA/CD** (with **Collision Detection**) fixes this: the sender **keeps listening while it transmits**, and as soon as it detects a collision it **stops transmitting**, so far less channel time is wasted.',
         'CSMA/CD was the access method of classic **shared Ethernet**: a bus cable, or a star topology built around a **hub** (a hub repeats every signal to every port, so it behaves like a shared bus). Modern switched, full-duplex Ethernet doesn’t need it (Chapter 4).'],
    18: ['How does a station know there was a collision? Its **transceiver** monitors the medium while transmitting. If the **power observed** on the medium is **higher than the power of its own signal**, someone else must be sending at the same time: a collision. In the figure, the observed signal has extra energy exactly where the other frame overlaps.'],
    19: ['CSMA/CD uses one of the three persistence methods (non-, 1- or p-persistent) to decide when to send. If a station **detects a collision** while sending: (1) it **aborts** its transmission; (2) it sends a short **jam signal** (48 bits in these slides), which makes sure **every** station, including the farthest one, notices the collision and **discards** the damaged frame.',
         '(3) It **backs off** for a **random** amount of time (random, so the colliding stations don’t retry at the same moment and collide again); (4) then it **tries again** using the same persistence method. Order to remember: abort, jam, back off, retry.'],
    20: ['Advantages of random access: it is **simple** and easy to implement; it is **decentralised**, so there is no central device whose failure would bring down the whole network; and when traffic is **low**, frames get through with **little delay**. The downside appears under **heavy traffic**: collisions multiply and **delay has no upper limit**.',
         'It is also an **unfair** protocol: nothing guarantees a turn, so a station may keep losing and **never** get to send. On the other hand, if a node is the **only** one with frames, it can send **continuously at the full channel rate R**: nobody is competing, so nothing slows it down.',
         'If **M** nodes all want to send, collisions eat into the capacity, so each node’s average throughput is **less than R/M**, the share it would get from a perfect equal split with no collisions or overhead.'],
    21: ['**Controlled access** (scheduling) gives stations **in-order access**: every station gets its turn, so it is a **fair** protocol. Because only one station sends at a time, it **eliminates collisions completely**. The trade-off: each node is limited to **R/M**, even if it is the only one with data, because it still has to wait for its turn. There are three methods: **reservation**, **polling** and **token passing**. Compare with random access: random can collide and is unfair, but a lone station gets all of R; controlled never collides and is fair, but caps everyone at R/M.'],
    22: ['In the **reservation** method, stations take turns sending one frame each at the **full rate R**. Time is organised into **cycles** of variable length.',
         'Each cycle starts with a **reservation frame** made of **N minislots**, one per station. A station that has a frame to send marks its own minislot (makes a reservation). In the figure, stations 1, 3 and 4 reserved in the first cycle.',
         'Every station listens to the reservation frame, so every station knows **who will send and in which order**. After the reservation frame, the stations that reserved send their data frames in that order. Then the next cycle starts with a new reservation frame. A cycle with fewer reservations is shorter, which is why the length varies.'],
    23: ['**Polling** has two forms. In **centralised polling**, one device is the **primary station** and the rest are **secondary stations**; all data goes through the primary, and secondaries only send when the primary allows them.',
         'When the primary wants to **send** to a secondary, it sends a **SELECT** frame with that secondary’s address (to check it is ready), and then the data. When the primary is ready to **receive**, it sends a **POLL** frame to each secondary in turn, asking whether it has data: the secondary replies with **data** if it has some, or with a **NAK** (negative acknowledgement) if it has nothing. Polling can go in round-robin order or a predetermined order. Memory aid: select = the primary sends; poll = the primary collects.',
         'In **distributed polling** there is **no primary**. Stations follow a known **polling order** (set by the protocol); the station with the highest priority sends first and then passes the right to send to the next station on the list, and so on. **Reservation** and **token passing** are both examples of distributed polling.'],
    24: ['The figure shows the two exchanges. **Select** (primary to secondary): the primary sends SEL, the secondary answers ACK (ready), the primary sends the data, and the secondary ACKs it. **Poll** (secondary to primary): the primary sends Poll; a secondary with nothing answers **NAK**; one with data sends it, and the primary answers ACK.'],
    25: ['**Token passing** is a distributed polling system. A special small frame called the **token** travels around a (logical) ring from station to station. A station that wants to send must **wait for a free token** to reach it, then **captures** it: marks it busy, attaches its data and the destination address. The destination copies the data and the token is set back to **free** so the next station can use it. Since only the token holder can send, there are **no collisions**.'],
    26: ['The procedure: wait for the token; capture it; send frames as long as there is **data left** and the station’s **allocated time hasn’t run out**; then **release the token** to the next station. The time limit stops one station from keeping the token forever, which is what makes token passing fair.'],
    27: ['**Channelization** shares the link’s bandwidth by splitting it between stations: in **frequency** (FDMA), in **time** (TDMA) or by **code** (CDMA). Each station gets its own channel, so stations don’t collide. It is mostly used in cellular and satellite systems.'],
    28: ['In **FDMA**, the available bandwidth is divided into frequency bands, and each station has **its own band, all the time**. It is like radio stations: each broadcasts on its own frequency at the same time, without interfering.'],
    29: ['**FDMA** (Frequency Division Multiple Access): the medium is divided into **M separate frequency bands**, one per station.',
         'Each station transmits **continuously** on its own band, at an average rate of **R/M**.',
         'The drawback: a station is limited to **R/M** even when it is **the only node with frames** to send. The other bands sit empty, but it isn’t allowed to use them.'],
    30: ['In **TDMA**, every station uses the **whole bandwidth**, but only during **its own time slot**; the slots repeat in a fixed cycle. It is like several people sharing one microphone, each allowed to speak for a fixed few seconds in turn.'],
    31: ['**TDMA** (Time Division Multiple Access): the entire bandwidth is one channel whose capacity is **shared in time** between **M** stations, each owning one slot per cycle.',
         'A node must **always wait for its own slot**, even when it is the only one with frames; the other slots go unused. So, like FDMA, it is limited to an average of **R/M** even when alone. Random access is the only family here that lets a lone station use all of R.'],
    32: ['**CDMA** (Code Division Multiple Access): **one channel** carries **all transmissions at the same time**, on the same frequencies. Each station **codes** its data with its own unique code before sending, and a receiver uses the sender’s code to **recover** that station’s data from the mixture. It is like a room where several pairs talk at once in different languages: you can follow your own conversation because you only understand your own language.'],
    33: ['How the codes work: each station multiplies its data d by its code c and sends d·c. The channel carries the **sum** of everything: d1·c1 + d2·c2 + d3·c3 + d4·c4. A receiver that wants station 1’s data multiplies the sum by **c1**; the codes are chosen so that the other stations’ parts cancel out, leaving d1. That is how simultaneous transmissions are separated.'],
    34: ['The three channelization methods side by side. **FDMA** slices the channel by **frequency** (each station has a band all the time). **TDMA** slices it by **time** (each station has all frequencies for its slot). **CDMA** slices it by **code** (every station uses all frequencies all the time, separated by codes).'],
}

